-- limit_ratio(1, up): the whole ratio, keeps all 6 series.
6
-- limit_ratio(0, up): empty ratio, keeps nothing.
0
-- limit_ratio(-1, up): the whole complement, keeps all 6 series.
6
-- limit_ratio(1.5, up): out-of-range r is clamped to 1, keeps all 6 series.
6
-- limit_ratio(NaN, up): a NaN ratio is rejected, as in Prometheus.
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
-- limit_ratio(scalar(up), up): a computed NaN ratio (scalar() of 6 series) is rejected too.
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
-- The same for a computed ratio which varies along the grid of a subquery.
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
-- A NaN ratio is rejected even if the vector is known to be empty when the query is built.
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
Ratio of aggregation operator limit_ratio must not be NaN
-- limit_ratio(1, clamp(up, 1, -1)): a valid ratio over such a vector gives an empty result.
0
-- Such an empty result keeps the evaluation time grid, in an instant and in a range query.
[]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	7
[]	[('2023-11-14 22:12:20.000',7),('2023-11-14 22:12:50.000',7),('2023-11-14 22:13:20.000',7)]
-- limit_ratio(0.5, up) and limit_ratio(-0.5, up) keep complementary, reproducible subsets.
r=0.5:  host2 host4 host6 
r=-0.5: host1 host3 host5 
-- limit_ratio keeps the original series labels (including the metric name).
[('__name__','up'),('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	1
